WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.380
hello everybody its Michelle from loud
muffin beauty science here you might have

00:00:04.380 --> 00:00:07.799
seen the news that Hawaii has banned
some sunscreens that have been linked to

00:00:07.799 --> 00:00:12.660
coral bleaching the two ingredients
are the organic or so-called chemical

00:00:12.660 --> 00:00:19.410
sunscreens oxybenzone and octinoxate. Are
these ingredients that bad? should you

00:00:19.410 --> 00:00:23.880
avoid them at the beach? should you avoid
them in your daily sunscreens? is the

00:00:23.880 --> 00:00:28.050
ban supported by the science? that's
what we're talking about today: the

00:00:28.050 --> 00:00:37.770
science behind sunscreen and coral. One
of the main issues facing coral is

00:00:37.770 --> 00:00:41.430
bleaching and that's the main harmful
effect that's been linked to some

00:00:41.430 --> 00:00:47.280
sunscreens coral bleaching happens when
the coral turns white the stuff that

00:00:47.280 --> 00:00:51.899
makes coral colored are microscopic algae
called zooxanthellae these algae and

00:00:51.899 --> 00:00:56.840
coral live in a symbiotic relationship
it's a beneficial live in partnership

00:00:56.840 --> 00:01:00.780
the algae live inside the coral and they
play an important role

00:01:00.780 --> 00:01:05.189
they absorb light from the Sun and
photosynthesise and the nutrients that

00:01:05.189 --> 00:01:10.409
they create provides the coral with
about 90% of its energy but when the

00:01:10.409 --> 00:01:14.189
coral gets stressed
that's when bleaching happens the algae

00:01:14.189 --> 00:01:19.290
leave the coral leaving the coral white
and bleached without the algae the coral

00:01:19.290 --> 00:01:23.150
don't have their main source of food and
so they'll slowly starve to death

00:01:23.150 --> 00:01:28.110
bleaching can be reversible if the
stress goes away then the algae can go

00:01:28.110 --> 00:01:32.759
back to the coral and everything is okay
again but if there's too much stress or

00:01:32.759 --> 00:01:37.259
if the stress goes on for too long then
the coral stays bleached and eventually

00:01:37.259 --> 00:01:41.180
it dies there are lots of different
stresses that can cause coral bleaching

00:01:41.180 --> 00:01:46.229
the main ones are temperature changes
particularly the higher temperatures

00:01:46.229 --> 00:01:48.990
that are associated with global warming
from climate change

00:01:48.990 --> 00:01:53.670
there's also ocean acidification when
the pH of the ocean drops because

00:01:53.670 --> 00:01:58.380
there's too much carbon dioxide again
this is related to climate change they

00:01:58.380 --> 00:02:02.939
might also be increased visible and UV
light from the Sun and also if there's

00:02:02.939 --> 00:02:07.619
too much pollution for example
herbicides fertilizers from farming oil

00:02:07.619 --> 00:02:10.580
spills and dirt

00:02:14.340 --> 00:02:19.000
relatively recently it's been discovered
that some sunscreen ingredients can also

00:02:19.000 --> 00:02:24.070
cause bleaching the studies were
conducted on isolated coral pieces of

00:02:24.070 --> 00:02:27.520
coral were placed in a bag and the
sunscreen ingredients were added to them

00:02:27.520 --> 00:02:32.110
here are some of the studies Danovaro
and co-workers found that hard corals

00:02:32.110 --> 00:02:37.000
were bleached by oxybenzone, octinoxate
which is also called octyl methoxy

00:02:37.000 --> 00:02:42.040
cinnamate or ethylhexyl methoxycinnamate
and enzacamene which is also known as

00:02:42.040 --> 00:02:47.770
4-methylbenzylidene camphor or 4-
MBC they also tested some other

00:02:47.770 --> 00:02:52.060
sunscreen ingredients and found that
they didn't cause any bleaching these

00:02:52.060 --> 00:02:57.490
sunscreens were octocrylene, octisalate
 or octyl salicylate and avobenzone

00:02:57.490 --> 00:03:03.940
a 2014 study found that titanium
dioxide nanoparticles could also cause

00:03:03.940 --> 00:03:09.760
slight bleaching a 2016 study from the
Haereticus laboratory by Downs and

00:03:09.760 --> 00:03:14.560
co-workers found that oxybenzone causes
bleaching and deformities in multiple

00:03:14.560 --> 00:03:20.980
coral species a 2017 study by Fel and
co-workers at L'Oreal found that octocrylene

00:03:20.980 --> 00:03:27.489
octyl triazone,  ecamsule or Mexoryl
 SX and drometrizole trisiloxane

00:03:27.489 --> 00:03:31.300
or Mexoryl XL had no effect on coral
while

00:03:31.300 --> 00:03:35.920
avobenzone only had an effect at a
very high concentrations. zinc oxide

00:03:35.920 --> 00:03:42.070
caused bleaching in this study. in 2018 a
study by Corinaldesi and co-workers

00:03:42.070 --> 00:03:46.600
found that uncoated zinc oxide
nanoparticles caused rapid bleaching

00:03:46.600 --> 00:03:51.489
while modified titanium dioxide
nanoparticles had a minimal effect I've

00:03:51.489 --> 00:03:54.670
put more details about each of these
studies in the blog post that I have

00:03:54.670 --> 00:03:58.150
linked to this video and the links to
each of the studies are in there as well

00:03:58.150 --> 00:04:03.160
it's in the caption now these studies
sound pretty worrying especially since

00:04:03.160 --> 00:04:06.519
the bleaching happened even with really
low concentrations of sunscreen

00:04:06.519 --> 00:04:09.670
ingredients we're talking parts per
million and parts per billion

00:04:09.670 --> 00:04:14.230
concentrations but the amounts of
sunscreen in the ocean are also really

00:04:14.230 --> 00:04:19.090
low so one of the key questions we
should be asking is is there enough

00:04:19.090 --> 00:04:23.370
sunscreen in the environment to harm
coral?

00:04:26.509 --> 00:04:30.720
first off we're going to have to talk
more about these extremely low

00:04:30.720 --> 00:04:34.919
concentrations
there's extremely low and then there's

00:04:34.919 --> 00:04:41.490
extremely extremely low here are some of
the numbers involved ppm means parts per

00:04:41.490 --> 00:04:47.580
million or milligrams per liter this is
the equivalent of about 1/50th of a drop

00:04:47.580 --> 00:04:52.409
diluted in one liter which is about a
quarter gallon for you US people it's

00:04:52.409 --> 00:04:58.259
about this much
ppb means parts per billion or

00:04:58.259 --> 00:05:05.639
micrograms per liter 1 ppb is a thousand
times more dilute than 1 ppm. ppt means

00:05:05.639 --> 00:05:11.669
parts per trillion or nano grams per
liter 1 ppt is a thousand times more

00:05:11.669 --> 00:05:17.880
dilute than 1 ppb and a million times
more dilute than 1 ppm a fundamental

00:05:17.880 --> 00:05:23.310
principle in toxicology or the science of
how bad stuff is is that the dose makes

00:05:23.310 --> 00:05:28.169
the poison the long version of this is
that everything can be a poison in large

00:05:28.169 --> 00:05:34.800
enough amounts and everything is safe in
low enough amounts ppm ppb and ppt are

00:05:34.800 --> 00:05:40.680
all extremely low and tiny to us but the
difference between ppm and ppt is 1

00:05:40.680 --> 00:05:47.430
million so to go from 1 ppm to 1 ppt you
take your liter with 1/50th of a drop and

00:05:47.430 --> 00:05:52.050
throw it into half of an Olympic
swimming pool this massive difference is

00:05:52.050 --> 00:05:54.840
really important when we're looking at
the amounts of sunscreen in the

00:05:54.840 --> 00:05:59.219
environment are the concentration is low
enough to be safe or are they high

00:05:59.219 --> 00:06:03.960
enough to be harmful? in these studies
the coal was exposed to sunscreen in the

00:06:03.960 --> 00:06:08.430
parts per million and parts per billion
range and while these are really low

00:06:08.430 --> 00:06:12.990
other coral researchers have pointed out
that these concentrations are actually

00:06:12.990 --> 00:06:16.590
much higher than the amounts of
sunscreen that you find in the vast

00:06:16.590 --> 00:06:21.300
majority of the environment for example
the lowest concentration of pure

00:06:21.300 --> 00:06:26.550
sunscreen filter used in the 2008 Danovaro paper that had an effect was about

00:06:26.550 --> 00:06:31.740
1 ppm of enzacamene or
4-MBC one part per million sounds

00:06:31.740 --> 00:06:35.520
really small but if you think about it
it actually turns out to be quite a lot

00:06:35.520 --> 00:06:38.879
if you apply
30 grams of sunscreen which is the

00:06:38.879 --> 00:06:44.400
recommended amount for an adult-sized
body and it has 3% enzacamene in it and

00:06:44.400 --> 00:06:49.469
all of it washed off your body into the
water, to get one part per million you'd

00:06:49.469 --> 00:06:54.680
be swimming in about 900 liters of water
which is only about three bathtubs full

00:06:54.680 --> 00:06:59.340
to get 2.28 parts per
billion of oxybenzone one of the lowest

00:06:59.340 --> 00:07:04.229
amounts tested in the 2016 Downs
study your 30 grams of sunscreen

00:07:04.229 --> 00:07:09.990
containing 6% oxybenzone would have to
completely wash off in 790,000 litres of

00:07:09.990 --> 00:07:14.789
water or about 1/3 of an Olympic
swimming pool these estimates are

00:07:14.789 --> 00:07:18.960
actually on the high side since studies
have found that people usually wear less

00:07:18.960 --> 00:07:22.529
than half the recommended amount of
sunscreen and the amount that dissolves

00:07:22.529 --> 00:07:27.479
in water is a lot less than 100% one
study estimated that only about a

00:07:27.479 --> 00:07:32.099
quarter of your sunscreen washes off
during a 20-minute swim the ocean is

00:07:32.099 --> 00:07:37.830
also incredibly incredibly massive
ridiculously unimaginably mind-blowingly

00:07:37.830 --> 00:07:44.250
massive it contains about 97% of the
world's water which is 1.34 times 10 to

00:07:44.250 --> 00:07:49.589
the 21 litres that's about 200 times
more liters than there are grains of

00:07:49.589 --> 00:07:55.020
sand in the world it's 70,000 Olympic
sized swimming pools for each of the 7.44

00:07:55.020 --> 00:08:00.330
billion people living on earth
there are also ocean currents that mix

00:08:00.330 --> 00:08:04.139
this water around so it shouldn't be
surprising that sunscreen is only found

00:08:04.139 --> 00:08:08.699
in almost undetectable concentrations in
most of the environment that's in the

00:08:08.699 --> 00:08:15.300
ppt range and a lot of the time it can't
actually be detected but in some

00:08:15.300 --> 00:08:19.379
situations in some environments there
could be higher concentrations of

00:08:19.379 --> 00:08:24.060
sunscreen that could realistically
affect coral generally this is in the

00:08:24.060 --> 00:08:28.229
more enclosed bays in areas with lots of
tourists where there might be hundreds

00:08:28.229 --> 00:08:33.209
of sunscreen covered people swimming in
a small area of water for example

00:08:33.209 --> 00:08:36.449
there's been high concentrations of
oxybenzone measure near popular beaches

00:08:36.449 --> 00:08:40.620
in the US Virgin Islands at Hawksnest
Bay and Trunk Bay

00:08:40.620 --> 00:08:46.079
75 parts per billion and 1.4 parts per
million of oxybenzone have been measured

00:08:46.079 --> 00:08:50.649
during busy times of the day there's
also a possibility that even though the amounts

00:08:50.649 --> 00:08:56.050
in water are low bioaccumulation might
happen this is when the coral takes in

00:08:56.050 --> 00:09:00.069
and collects the sunscreen ingredients
so they build up and form a much higher

00:09:00.069 --> 00:09:09.369
concentration inside the coral so there
are only really low concentrations in

00:09:09.369 --> 00:09:13.059
the environment although in some places
there might be higher but there's

00:09:13.059 --> 00:09:17.410
another big question mark in the data
there isn't any solid evidence of

00:09:17.410 --> 00:09:21.519
sunscreens having caused harm to coral
reefs yet some researchers have

00:09:21.519 --> 00:09:25.300
mentioned that they've seen that coral
reefs in a few areas with more swimmers

00:09:25.300 --> 00:09:29.740
are more degraded than in areas with
less swimmers or they're worse in areas

00:09:29.740 --> 00:09:34.449
with more sunscreen use but this is
anecdotal evidence at the moment so it

00:09:34.449 --> 00:09:38.199
isn't really convincing there could be
other factors at plays so for example

00:09:38.199 --> 00:09:41.889
the tourists might trample on the coral
reefs so all the headlines that you

00:09:41.889 --> 00:09:45.819
might have read that sunscreen kills
coral reefs oxybenzone causes major

00:09:45.819 --> 00:09:50.589
damage to coral reefs the sunscreen ban
will save coral reefs this isn't what

00:09:50.589 --> 00:09:54.999
the studies say some scientists have
tried to estimate the amount of damage

00:09:54.999 --> 00:09:59.740
that sunscreens might cause to coral in
aquatic recreational areas in Hong Kong

00:09:59.740 --> 00:10:04.089
near snorkeling hotspots it's been
estimated that the risk of bleaching by

00:10:04.089 --> 00:10:11.019
oxybenzone is 21% and the risk of
bleaching by octinoxate is 11% a 2008

00:10:11.019 --> 00:10:15.309
study estimated that 10% of the world's
reefs are threatened by sunscreen

00:10:15.309 --> 00:10:19.959
pollution and this numbers been cited in
a bunch of other papers this sounds

00:10:19.959 --> 00:10:24.370
pretty concerning but marine scientists
who research the wider causes of coral

00:10:24.370 --> 00:10:33.970
reef damage say not so fast
a lot of marine scientist are actually pretty

00:10:33.970 --> 00:10:38.890
critical of the sunscreen ban if we zoom
out and look at the wider picture it

00:10:38.890 --> 00:10:43.090
turns out that the possible harm from
sunscreens is really really tiny in

00:10:43.090 --> 00:10:47.770
recent textbooks and reviews on coral
bleaching sunscreens don't even rate a

00:10:47.770 --> 00:10:52.750
mention instead there's tons and tons of
data showing that climate change and

00:10:52.750 --> 00:10:57.430
agricultural management have wreaked
havoc on coral reefs and will continue

00:10:57.430 --> 00:11:00.220
to do so
professor Terry Hughes a massively

00:11:00.220 --> 00:11:04.600
respected coral expert and the director
of the ARC center of excellence for

00:11:04.600 --> 00:11:09.220
coral reef studies says he would put
sunscreen all the way down and number

00:11:09.220 --> 00:11:14.260
200 on a list of bad things that humans
can do to coral reefs he points out that

00:11:14.260 --> 00:11:18.550
the worst coral bleaching is happening
in areas where tourists don't go so for

00:11:18.550 --> 00:11:23.080
example in the massive 2016 Great
Barrier Reef bleaching event the worst

00:11:23.080 --> 00:11:27.670
bleaching by far happened in the remote
northern region where and the severe

00:11:27.670 --> 00:11:31.420
bleaching patterns matched the areas
with higher water temperatures higher

00:11:31.420 --> 00:11:34.080
water temperatures caused by climate
change

00:11:34.080 --> 00:11:38.650
researchers Kelvin Gorospe and Austin
Humphries at the University of Rhode

00:11:38.650 --> 00:11:42.790
Island also say that the damage from
sunscreen is negligible when you look at

00:11:42.790 --> 00:11:46.600
how climate change threatens coral reefs
they say that if you want to do

00:11:46.600 --> 00:11:49.870
something to save the reefs in the ocean
you shouldn't be worrying about

00:11:49.870 --> 00:11:54.310
sunscreen banning sunscreens to try to
save coral reefs is a bit like

00:11:54.310 --> 00:11:58.210
rearranging deck chairs on the Titanic
or polishing a scratch on your doorknob

00:11:58.210 --> 00:12:03.640
when your house is burning down yes
technically every little bit helps

00:12:03.640 --> 00:12:08.290
but is it worth spending time and effort
and resources dealing with sunscreen

00:12:08.290 --> 00:12:12.580
when there are much much bigger issues
to work on and many of these scientists

00:12:12.580 --> 00:12:16.240
think that it's actually a bit of a
sneaky trick by politicians to hype up

00:12:16.240 --> 00:12:20.620
the sunscreen issue it's an easy way for
them to show us that they're doing

00:12:20.620 --> 00:12:24.130
something while distracting us from the
real issues climate change and

00:12:24.130 --> 00:12:28.000
agricultural management which are much
harder to fix and something that their

00:12:28.000 --> 00:12:31.570
corporate donors probably don't want
them to fix because it cuts into their

00:12:31.570 --> 00:12:35.830
profits so they encourage us to think
that sunscreens are much bigger issue

00:12:35.830 --> 00:12:41.350
than they actually are so that we might
get off their backs and ironically if

00:12:41.350 --> 00:12:44.470
you're throwing out unused sunscreens
and driving around looking for the right

00:12:44.470 --> 00:12:48.040
sunscreens with the right ingredients
you're actually making the climate

00:12:48.040 --> 00:12:58.450
change problem worse okay so what can we
actually do to help the reefs firstly in

00:12:58.450 --> 00:13:01.900
terms of sunscreen you don't really need
to do much except when you're planning

00:13:01.900 --> 00:13:06.760
to swim in an area close to coral if
that's the case you should try to use

00:13:06.760 --> 00:13:10.780
more sun protective clothing and stay in
the shade more so that you can use a bit

00:13:10.780 --> 00:13:15.010
less sunscreen for the bits of your skin
that are still exposed you should look

00:13:15.010 --> 00:13:17.830
for sunscreens that don't have
ingredients that have been found to be

00:13:17.830 --> 00:13:21.430
harmful to coral the common sunscreen
ingredients that have been found to be

00:13:21.430 --> 00:13:28.540
harmful are oxybenzone, octinoxate,
enzacamene or 4-MBC and zinc oxide the

00:13:28.540 --> 00:13:32.140
common sunscreen ingredients that have
been found to cause minimal harm or no

00:13:32.140 --> 00:13:39.940
harm at all are octocrylene octisalate, avobenzone
octyl triazone, ecamsule

00:13:39.940 --> 00:13:43.540
or Mexoryl SX
drometrizole trisiloxane or

00:13:43.540 --> 00:13:48.130
Mexoryl XL and titanium dioxide the
sunscreen ingredients that haven't been

00:13:48.130 --> 00:13:55.030
tested on coral yet are bemotrizinol or
Tinosorb S bisoctrizole or Tinosorb M

00:13:55.030 --> 00:14:01.000
DHHB or Uvinul A Plus ensulizole
and homosalate there are lots of

00:14:01.000 --> 00:14:04.720
brands that have coral safe sunscreens
in their range even if they don't say

00:14:04.720 --> 00:14:10.090
coral safe so for example Supergoop
La Roche Posay Sunsense Stream2Sea

00:14:10.090 --> 00:14:14.800
Coppertone and Headhunter it's
important that you actually check the

00:14:14.800 --> 00:14:18.250
ingredients of the sunscreen and don't
just look for labels that say reef

00:14:18.250 --> 00:14:22.510
friendly or biodegradable these terms
aren't regulated so they don't

00:14:22.510 --> 00:14:26.350
necessarily mean anything so there's a
lot of sunscreens that say they're reef

00:14:26.350 --> 00:14:31.210
friendly but might contain oxybenzone a
lot of sunscreen brands also assume that

00:14:31.210 --> 00:14:34.390
all organic or chemical sunscreens are
harmful

00:14:34.390 --> 00:14:38.590
while all inorganic or mineral
sunscreens are safe even though zinc

00:14:38.590 --> 00:14:42.430
oxide has been found to be worse for
coral than many chemical sunscreens I

00:14:42.430 --> 00:14:46.420
talk a little bit more about why I
think that it's not just nanoparticle

00:14:46.420 --> 00:14:50.260
zinc oxide that harms coral in my blog
post so check that out if you're

00:14:50.260 --> 00:14:53.960
interested in the nerdy nitty gritty
biodegradable also

00:14:53.960 --> 00:14:58.370
doesn't mean reef safe both zinc oxide
and titanium dioxide are non

00:14:58.370 --> 00:15:03.320
biodegradable since living organisms
can't break them down but zinc oxide has

00:15:03.320 --> 00:15:07.430
been found to be harmful to coral in
some studies while titanium dioxide has

00:15:07.430 --> 00:15:11.450
been relatively safe you should try to
limit how much sunscreen comes off your

00:15:11.450 --> 00:15:15.740
body into the water as well look for a
highly water-resistant sunscreen and

00:15:15.740 --> 00:15:20.090
apply your sunscreen 20 minutes before
you go into the water so it has a chance

00:15:20.090 --> 00:15:22.310
to dry down and stick to your skin
better

00:15:22.310 --> 00:15:26.060
the other big thing we should all be
doing for coral and for the rest of the

00:15:26.060 --> 00:15:30.460
environment is to do something about
climate change

00:15:31.870 --> 00:15:36.130
climate change is a huge and scary
problem but there are some things that

00:15:36.130 --> 00:15:40.660
we can all do to help we can try to cut
down on our biggest contributors to our

00:15:40.660 --> 00:15:45.550
own carbon footprints for most people
this is owning and driving a car going

00:15:45.550 --> 00:15:50.440
on long flights and eating meat you can
also switch to an energy company that

00:15:50.440 --> 00:15:55.390
uses green energy you can also vote for
political parties who firstly recognize

00:15:55.390 --> 00:15:59.500
that climate change is a problem and who
have policies that actually tackle

00:15:59.500 --> 00:16:03.730
climate change and you can contact your
local representatives about climate

00:16:03.730 --> 00:16:08.380
change if you feel like your voice isn't
that important and you're just one small

00:16:08.380 --> 00:16:12.520
person it might make you feel better to
know that because most people don't

00:16:12.520 --> 00:16:17.110
contact them they assume that for every
one person who does contact there are

00:16:17.110 --> 00:16:22.210
ten or twenty silent people who feel the
same way and that's all for today sorry

